\subsection{Robustness and Boundary Conditions}
\label{sec:robustness}

Across three seeds at $\rho = 0.6$, adaptive $\bstar$ ($17.736 \pm 0.004$) outperforms uniform on in-distribution PPL ($17.763 \pm 0.008$, $\Delta{=}{-}0.028$, directionally consistent across all seeds), a diminished effect compared to $\rho{=}0.4$ ($\Delta{=}{-}0.010$ from-scratch).
The ablation hierarchy is preserved (Grid 17.634\footnote{Single seed; multi-seed confirmation pending.} ${<}$ Adaptive 17.736 ${<}$ Uniform 17.763 ${\approx}$ ESS-only 17.763 ${<}$ Random 17.861), but the ESS-only/uniform gap collapses to effectively zero (three-seed means both $17.763$) as ESS binding rises to ${\sim}$66\%.
Random remains substantially worse than uniform (gap 0.098, $\sigma{=}0.025$): detector signal is load-bearing even when the stability constraint dominates.
The adaptive method's continued advantage is driven by the $\aeff$ quality constraint, which operates independently of the ESS ceiling.
A $\tau$ sweep at this level finds $\tau{=}0.7$ optimal (PPL 17.730 vs.\ 17.740 for default $\tau{=}0.8$); $\tau \in [0.7, 0.8]$ performs well across tested $\rho$ (Appendix~\ref{app:tau_rho}).
Full per-seed results and degradation analysis are in Appendix~\ref{app:rho06}.

\paragraph{Scale (preliminary).}
At 1.7B parameters (Qwen3-1.7B, $\rho{=}0.4$), adaptive $\bstar$ achieves PPL $13.26 \pm 0.01$ (2 seeds: 13.25, 13.27) vs.\ 13.26 for uniform (1 from-scratch seed). With only 2 seeds and no grid-best comparison, these results are insufficient for statistical conclusions about scale transfer; we find no evidence of either improvement or degradation ($\Delta{=}{-}0.01$).
\paragraph{Out-of-distribution evaluation.}
To test whether the in-distribution improvement reflects distribution matching, we evaluate all three from-scratch seeds on OpenWebText and Wikipedia.
Adaptive reweighting yields small, consistent improvements across all domains: FineWeb $\Delta{=}{-}0.011$, OpenWebText $\Delta{=}{-}0.004$, Wikipedia $\Delta{=}{-}0.002$ (3-seed means; downstream accuracy identical at 0.5414).
We find no evidence of distribution matching; the improvement, while small ($|\Delta| < 0.01$ OOD), is directionally consistent across seeds and evaluation corpora.
At the recommended $\tau{=}0.7$, OOD improvements are larger ($\Delta{=}{-}0.028$ OWT, $\Delta{=}{-}0.029$ Wiki; \S\ref{sec:sensitivity}).

\paragraph{Detector signal type matters more than accuracy.}
Replacing the discriminative TF-IDF+LR with generative perplexity detectors reverses the advantage even at comparable AUC (GPT-2 PPL: AUC\,=\,0.91, $\Delta{=}{+}0.10$; N-gram PPL: AUC\,=\,0.73, $\Delta{=}{+}0.13$; 3 detectors, single seed; Appendix~\ref{app:detector}).
Generative detectors assign high $q$-scores to fluent human text, systematically biasing $\aeff$ and causing the method to suppress useful training signal.


\subsection{Hyperparameter Sensitivity}
\label{sec:sensitivity}

The adaptive method has two hyperparameters: quality threshold $\tau$ and ESS floor $\nmin$.

\paragraph{Quality threshold $\tau$.}
Three-seed from-scratch validation at $\rho{=}0.4$ finds $\tau{=}0.7$ marginally preferred ($17.674 \pm 0.007$, $\Delta{=}{-}0.019$ vs.\ uniform) over $\tau{=}0.8$ ($17.683 \pm 0.006$, $\Delta{=}{-}0.010$), consistent across all seeds.
The advantage extends to OOD evaluation: $\tau{=}0.7$ improves over uniform by $\Delta{=}{-}0.028$ (OpenWebText) and $\Delta{=}{-}0.029$ (Wikipedia), larger than the in-distribution improvement ($\Delta{=}{-}0.019$), with $\tau{=}0.7$ outperforming $\tau{=}0.8$ in all 9 seed$\times$domain comparisons (Table~\ref{tab:tau}).
This provides additional evidence against distribution matching: the improvement is not confined to the training distribution.
An exploratory single-seed sweep (Table~\ref{tab:tau}, Appendix~\ref{app:tau_sweep}) shows $\tau \in [0.5, 0.8]$ improves over uniform; beyond $\tau{=}0.8$, $\bstar$ inflates (mean 2.36 at $\tau{=}0.9$, 3.08 at $\tau{=}0.95$) and PPL degrades past uniform.

\paragraph{ESS floor $\nmin$.}
$\nmin{=}1$ (no stability) yields mean $\bstar{=}7.20$ and PPL 17.789; $\nmin{=}4$ ($= B$) forces $\bstar{=}0$, recovering uniform.
The default $\nmin{=}2$ ($\nmin/B{=}0.5$) is optimal and transfers across batch sizes ($B \in \{4, 8, 16\}$, differences ${<}0.01$).

Batch-adaptive $\tau$ yields no improvement over fixed $\tau$ (single-seed exploratory); both hyperparameters have default values that perform well without per-model tuning.

